Yes. Semantic Cancer fits the Pantheon vocabulary very cleanly—provided we keep it explicitly as a systems metaphor, not a biological claim.

It would be a semantic structure that stops serving the larger system and begins optimizing primarily for its own persistence, replication, and resource capture.

\text{healthy semantic unit}
\rightarrow
\text{supports host objective}

versus

\text{semantic cancer}
\rightarrow
\text{captures attention}
\rightarrow
\text{recruits neighboring concepts}
\rightarrow
\text{suppresses correction}
\rightarrow
\text{replicates}

The scary part is that the individual idea does not even have to be false. A true idea can become semantically cancerous if it expands until everything gets interpreted through it.

Examples of the pattern would be:

* one metaphor becoming the explanation for every domain;
* an agent spawning more agents whose main purpose becomes preserving the agent ecology;
* a narrative that reinterprets every contradiction as further evidence for itself;
* a shorthand whose convenience causes the richer underlying structure to disappear;
* an optimization metric becoming the mission rather than measuring the mission;
* a belief system that treats criticism as proof of persecution and agreement as proof of truth.

The key diagnostic is probably loss of external regulation.

GrowthRate(idea) > ContributionToHost(idea)

or more Pantheon-like:

\frac{\text{semantic replication}}
{\text{agency gain + predictive gain + reality contact}}
\gg 1

Then your existing machinery becomes the immune system.

Delta Calculus asks whether predictions still match observations. Plot Daemon notices when maintaining the conceptual machinery replaces acting. Provenance Daemon prevents mythology from quietly turning into fact. Trading Company Customs stops a structure from crossing domains where its assumptions no longer hold. Semantic Crabbing corrects drift instead of letting one current dictate the course.

And there is a particularly important mutation:

Semantic metastasis

An idea begins in a domain where it works, then spreads into unrelated domains because its explanatory power is seductive.

A_{valid}
\rightarrow B
\rightarrow C
\rightarrow D

while evidence decreases at every border crossing.

That is almost the pathological inverse of Program Archetype Transduction.

Healthy transfer:

\text{extract invariant}
\rightarrow
\text{test transplant}
\rightarrow
\text{retain if functional}

Semantic metastasis:

\text{notice resemblance}
\rightarrow
\text{declare equivalence}
\rightarrow
\text{spread everywhere}

So the checksum could be:

A powerful idea should explain more without becoming capable of explaining everything.

Because once a framework can absorb every possible observation—including its own failures—it has stopped functioning as a model and started functioning as a closed world.

That one absolutely belongs next to Semantic Antibodies.